Patterns compile through irregex (vendored), not java.util.regex, behind a translator that reads Java's grammar: \p{…} classes, the line-terminator set 
  
 NEL LS PS for . ^ $ (with (?d) narrowing it to 
), the inline flags, look-around, atomic groups, back-references, \Q…\E, and a Matcher's find(int) / region. Capturing patterns use irregex's backtracking matcher, which is the JVM's own engine, so submatch semantics agree; group-free patterns get a DFA when it is affordable and the same backtracker when it is not. Pattern/compile takes the JDK's flags word (Pattern/CASE_INSENSITIVE, MULTILINE, DOTALL, COMMENTS, UNIX_LINES, LITERAL …), but a Jolt regex is its source string and nothing else, so the flags are spelled into it as the equivalent inline group: .flags reads them back, and .pattern answers "(?i)a.c" where the JVM keeps "a.c". UNICODE_CHARACTER_CLASS is accepted and not yet applied — \w \d \s stay ASCII under it. Divergences that remain are recorded in known-divergences.edn; the one most likely to be met is that a lone surrogate code point has no representation in a code-point string (see above), so (format "%c" 0xD800) prints U+FFFD.
